WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.560
Instructor: So now let's talk about what we do

00:01.560 --> 00:04.717
or what we can do in this detached HEAD state.

00:04.717 --> 00:09.451
So in my repo, I've gone back to what is this commit again?

00:09.451 --> 00:13.110
4f1, split chapter one into two pieces,

00:13.110 --> 00:14.781
I'll also show you in GitKraken.

00:14.781 --> 00:16.517
This is where I went back to.

00:16.517 --> 00:19.848
It shows me HEAD is pointing at this one commit.

00:19.848 --> 00:22.380
Master branch is still all the way up here,

00:22.380 --> 00:24.390
that branch reference pointing over here,

00:24.390 --> 00:25.350
begin chapter three.

00:25.350 --> 00:26.280
There's like what?

00:26.280 --> 00:29.310
Eight commits or so in the meantime or in between.

00:29.310 --> 00:33.000
But I am now just looking at this split chapter one

00:33.000 --> 00:34.830
into two pieces commit.

00:34.830 --> 00:38.730
Okay, so one thing that I can do is just poke around,

00:38.730 --> 00:41.370
examine the contents of the old commit, view the files,

00:41.370 --> 00:44.681
maybe copy something and then bring it with me somewhere.

00:44.681 --> 00:47.520
We can see that all my files have been updated

00:47.520 --> 00:50.373
to reflect the fact that we're all the way back here.

00:51.270 --> 00:55.380
So I can see Gatsby everywhere.

00:55.380 --> 00:57.060
I don't see chapter three.

00:57.060 --> 01:00.420
There's work that is missing because I've gone back in time.

01:00.420 --> 01:02.850
So if that's all I wanted to do, great.

01:02.850 --> 01:05.340
Now if I want to go back to where I was,

01:05.340 --> 01:07.170
I wanna go back to master,

01:07.170 --> 01:09.720
all I need to do is switch to a branch.

01:09.720 --> 01:11.640
So if I have a detached HEAD,

01:11.640 --> 01:13.260
I can switch back to master,

01:13.260 --> 01:15.780
Git switch master or Git checkout master,

01:15.780 --> 01:17.214
and we're reattached.

01:17.214 --> 01:18.380
So I can show that.

01:18.380 --> 01:21.390
We've got only one branch in this repo,

01:21.390 --> 01:23.910
so master, I'll just switch back.

01:23.910 --> 01:25.846
So right now I'm in detached HEAD.

01:25.846 --> 01:29.853
Now if I do switch master, Git switch master,

01:31.620 --> 01:34.012
you can see I'm not in detached HEAD.

01:34.012 --> 01:36.900
We're back where master is, right?

01:36.900 --> 01:38.460
HEAD points to master.

01:38.460 --> 01:40.320
If I type Git log,

01:40.320 --> 01:42.600
we can see we have all those commits again,

01:42.600 --> 01:46.080
and HEAD is pointing to that master branch reference.

01:46.080 --> 01:49.380
So nothing changed, we didn't mess up anything.

01:49.380 --> 01:51.660
We simply went back as an observer,

01:51.660 --> 01:55.320
we took a look at what happened all those commits ago,

01:55.320 --> 01:58.590
we time traveled back and then time traveled forward.

01:58.590 --> 02:00.720
But if I wanted to go back,

02:00.720 --> 02:05.400
let's say to where I finished chapter one,

02:05.400 --> 02:07.560
and then I wanted to split off

02:07.560 --> 02:10.710
and try a new version of chapter two and chapter three,

02:10.710 --> 02:13.050
basically I want to get rid of all of this work.

02:13.050 --> 02:14.790
I'm not gonna delete all of that work,

02:14.790 --> 02:17.550
but I'm gonna try making a new branch

02:17.550 --> 02:19.050
that doesn't include any of this.

02:19.050 --> 02:23.400
So I'll go back in time maybe to this commit right here,

02:23.400 --> 02:27.600
whatever this hash is, and then I will branch off

02:27.600 --> 02:29.850
and do some stuff on that new branch.

02:29.850 --> 02:33.360
So I'll make a branch based upon this position,

02:33.360 --> 02:34.830
this particular commit.

02:34.830 --> 02:36.600
So I won't lose all this stuff,

02:36.600 --> 02:38.700
I'm just gonna make a new branch without it.

02:38.700 --> 02:39.870
Okay?

02:39.870 --> 02:42.210
So that's the third option, really I guess,

02:42.210 --> 02:43.560
for what we can do here,

02:43.560 --> 02:45.570
create a new branch and switch to it.

02:45.570 --> 02:47.838
Now I can make, I can save changes, I can make commits,

02:47.838 --> 02:51.697
because HEAD will now be attached to a new branch.

02:51.697 --> 02:54.543
So let's go back, let's pick one of those commits.

02:55.410 --> 02:58.308
Let's go to when I finished chapter one

02:58.308 --> 03:01.080
before we had chapter two.

03:01.080 --> 03:03.005
So I'm gonna copy that commit hash,

03:03.005 --> 03:05.730
make sure I don't have any un-staged changes

03:05.730 --> 03:06.780
and nothing to commit.

03:06.780 --> 03:07.860
Great.

03:07.860 --> 03:12.030
And I'll check out that old commit from a long time ago.

03:12.030 --> 03:14.730
Once again, detached HEAD, we can see where I am.

03:14.730 --> 03:16.830
I'm all the way back here.

03:16.830 --> 03:19.290
Now what I'm gonna do is make a new branch.

03:19.290 --> 03:21.000
So just like I make any other branch,

03:21.000 --> 03:23.580
I mean I can make it first, Git branch blah,

03:23.580 --> 03:26.370
and then switch to it or just do it in one go.

03:26.370 --> 03:28.040
What should I call this branch?

03:28.040 --> 03:31.147
Let's call it, sure,

03:31.147 --> 03:36.147
chapter two redo, like that, okay?

03:36.366 --> 03:39.416
So I made a new branch and I switched to it

03:39.416 --> 03:41.567
and just like anytime we make a branch,

03:41.567 --> 03:44.490
that branch is based upon HEAD.

03:44.490 --> 03:47.670
And at that point in time, HEAD was this commit.

03:47.670 --> 03:50.760
So now we have a new branch here, chapter two redo,

03:50.760 --> 03:52.415
and I'm reattached,

03:52.415 --> 03:55.530
HEAD is pointing to an actual branch reference.

03:55.530 --> 03:56.730
We're not in detached HEAD.

03:56.730 --> 03:58.200
I typed Git status

03:58.200 --> 04:00.000
and we can start doing more work.

04:00.000 --> 04:02.550
So my files still look like they did

04:02.550 --> 04:06.660
when I was in detached HEAD, but now I am reattached.

04:06.660 --> 04:09.450
So I could make my own version of chapter two, right?

04:09.450 --> 04:11.400
Chapter two dot TXT,

04:11.400 --> 04:14.490
and just put some random stuff in here.

04:14.490 --> 04:16.305
Here's my chapter two.

04:16.305 --> 04:20.250
Maybe I want to, ah, we'll just leave it at that I guess.

04:20.250 --> 04:22.770
Maybe I'll delete all of chapter one.

04:22.770 --> 04:23.880
I'll empty that out.

04:23.880 --> 04:27.569
Whatever I do, Git status, I'll add those changes.

04:27.569 --> 04:30.990
I'm on this branch, chapter two redo,

04:30.990 --> 04:33.100
and I'll do something like start

04:35.340 --> 04:38.613
second stab at chapter two.

04:39.515 --> 04:41.772
So now I have this new commit

04:41.772 --> 04:45.600
and it only exists on my chapter two redo branch.

04:45.600 --> 04:47.760
So this branch has the first three commits

04:47.760 --> 04:50.340
from master and then a whole new one where I branched off

04:50.340 --> 04:53.310
and tried a completely different version of chapter two.

04:53.310 --> 04:56.820
I don't have this history, but it's still there

04:56.820 --> 04:58.890
and I could just switch back to master, right?

04:58.890 --> 05:00.030
So this is what I'm looking at here.

05:00.030 --> 05:01.410
Chapter one is empty.

05:01.410 --> 05:03.120
Chapter two is almost empty.

05:03.120 --> 05:05.804
There is no chapter three, but I can switch back,

05:05.804 --> 05:08.421
Git switch master at any point,

05:08.421 --> 05:11.130
and all that stuff is restored.

05:11.130 --> 05:14.010
I've gone back to where we were before.

05:14.010 --> 05:16.320
Okay, so that is pretty much it

05:16.320 --> 05:18.570
for checking out previous commits,

05:18.570 --> 05:21.810
Git checkout and then a commit hash will take us back

05:21.810 --> 05:23.340
but we'll be in detached HEAD.

05:23.340 --> 05:25.405
We can poke around, we can switch back,

05:25.405 --> 05:28.350
go back to master or whatever branch we want

05:28.350 --> 05:29.820
or we can create a new branch

05:29.820 --> 05:31.530
based upon the particular commit

05:31.530 --> 05:34.023
we've time traveled back to, okay?
